EVEN = [
("Maharana Pratap", "Maharana Pratap, the brave ruler of Mewar, was born on 9 May 1540 at Kumbhalgarh in Rajasthan. He belonged to the Sisodia dynasty of Rajputs and became the ruler of Mewar after the death of his father, Udai Singh. At a time when many rulers had accepted the authority of the Mughal emperor Akbar, Maharana Pratap refused to surrender the freedom of his land. In 1576, his forces fought the Mughal army, led by Raja Man Singh, in the famous Battle of Haldighati. Although he was outnumbered, he fought with great courage, and his loyal horse, Chetak, carried him to safety even after being badly wounded. After the battle, he lived for years in the hills and forests, often facing hunger and hardship, but he never gave up. With the help of the Bhil tribes and his faithful minister Bhamashah, he rebuilt his army and recovered most of Mewar. He died in 1597 at Chavand. He remains a symbol of self respect and patriotism."),
("Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj", "Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj, the founder of the Maratha kingdom, was born on 19 February 1630 at the Shivneri fort near Pune. His mother, Jijabai, taught him stories from the Ramayana and the Mahabharata and filled him with courage and love for his people. While still a teenager, he began capturing forts in the Sahyadri hills and laid the foundation of Swarajya, or self rule. He was a master of guerrilla warfare and used the hilly terrain cleverly against much larger armies. His escape from Agra in 1666, where he had been placed under house arrest by Aurangzeb, is one of the most daring episodes in Indian history. He was crowned Chhatrapati at Raigad in 1674. Shivaji was also an able administrator. He organised a council of eight ministers, built a strong navy to protect the coast and treated people of all faiths with respect. He passed away in 1680, but his ideals continued to inspire the Maratha power for generations."),
("Guru Tegh Bahadur", "Guru Tegh Bahadur was the ninth Guru of the Sikhs. He was born in 1621 in Amritsar and was the youngest son of Guru Hargobind. As a young man, he showed great courage in battle, and his name, which means mighty of the sword, reflects this bravery. Yet he was also a deeply spiritual person who spent long years in meditation. He became the Guru in 1664 and travelled widely to spread the message of truth, devotion and service. He founded the town of Anandpur Sahib. His hymns are included in the Guru Granth Sahib. When Kashmiri Pandits came to him seeking protection from forced religious conversion under the Mughal emperor Aurangzeb, he decided to stand up for their freedom of faith. He was arrested and taken to Delhi, where he refused to give up his beliefs. He was executed at Chandni Chowk in 1675. The Gurdwara Sis Ganj Sahib stands at that place, and he is remembered as Hind di Chadar, the protector of India."),
("Sant Ravidas", "Sant Ravidas was one of the most important saint poets of the Bhakti movement. He is believed to have been born in the fifteenth century near Varanasi in a family of leather workers, a community that was then treated as low by society. Despite facing discrimination, he rose to become a respected spiritual teacher whose followers included people of every caste. Ravidas taught that God lives in the heart of every human being and that true devotion does not depend on birth, rituals or pilgrimage. He dreamt of an ideal society, which he called Begampura, a city without sorrow, where there would be no discrimination, no taxes and no poverty. His simple and touching verses spread from village to village, and forty of his hymns were later included in the Guru Granth Sahib. Mirabai is said to have regarded him as her guru. His birth anniversary, Ravidas Jayanti, falls on Magh Purnima and is observed with great devotion, especially in Punjab and Uttar Pradesh."),
("The Indian Coast Guard", "The Indian Coast Guard was formally established on 18 August 1978 as an armed force to protect India's maritime interests in peacetime. It functions under the Ministry of Defence and works closely with the Navy, customs, fisheries and state police. Its main duties include protecting offshore installations, preventing smuggling and poaching, enforcing maritime laws and protecting the marine environment from oil spills. The Coast Guard is also the national agency for search and rescue at sea. Every year, its ships, aircraft and hovercraft save the lives of hundreds of fishermen, sailors and passengers caught in storms or accidents. During cyclones, it warns fishing boats to return to the shore and rescues those who are stranded. Its motto, Vayam Rakshamah, means we protect. Over the years, the force has grown into one of the largest coast guards in the world, with a fleet that includes patrol vessels, interceptor boats and modern surveillance aircraft. Coast Guard Day is observed on 1 February."),
("Sarojini Naidu", "Sarojini Naidu, known as the Nightingale of India, was born on 13 February 1879 in Hyderabad. She was a brilliant student and began writing poems at a young age. She studied in England, and her collections of poetry, such as The Golden Threshold and The Bird of Time, won praise for their beautiful imagery and musical language. Inspired by Gopal Krishna Gokhale and Mahatma Gandhi, she joined the freedom struggle and became one of its most powerful speakers. In 1925, she became the first Indian woman to serve as President of the Indian National Congress. She took an active part in the Salt Satyagraha and led the raid on the Dharasana salt works after Gandhi's arrest. She was imprisoned several times by the British. She also worked for the education of women and their right to vote. After Independence, she became the Governor of the United Provinces, now Uttar Pradesh, the first woman governor of an Indian state. Her birthday is celebrated as National Women's Day."),
("Jhalkari Bai", "Jhalkari Bai was a brave woman soldier who fought alongside Rani Lakshmibai of Jhansi in the revolt of 1857. She was born on 22 November 1830 in Bhojla, a village near Jhansi, in a poor family. As a young girl, she learnt horse riding and the use of weapons, and stories are told of how she once fought off a leopard with only an axe. She married Puran Kori, a soldier in the army of Jhansi, and later joined the Durga Dal, the women's wing of the Rani's army. Jhalkari Bai closely resembled Rani Lakshmibai. During the siege of Jhansi in 1858, when the British forces were about to capture the fort, she disguised herself as the Rani and went to the enemy camp. This clever move confused the British and gave the real Rani valuable time to escape safely from the fort. Her loyalty and sacrifice are remembered with great pride in Bundelkhand, and the government has issued a postal stamp in her honour."),
("Tantia Tope", "Tantia Tope was one of the most skilful generals of the revolt of 1857. His real name was Ramchandra Pandurang Tope, and he was born around 1814 in a Marathi family. He was a close companion of Nana Sahib, the adopted son of the last Peshwa, Baji Rao II. When the revolt broke out, Tantia Tope took a leading role in the fighting at Kanpur. Later, he went to the help of Rani Lakshmibai at Jhansi, and together they captured the important fort of Gwalior. After the Rani died fighting in June 1858, Tantia Tope continued the struggle almost alone. For nearly a year, he moved swiftly through Rajasthan and central India, fighting a guerrilla war and escaping the British forces again and again. His speed and knowledge of the land amazed even his enemies. Finally, he was betrayed by a trusted companion and captured in the forests in April 1859. He was tried and hanged at Shivpuri on 18 April 1859. He is remembered as a hero who never surrendered."),
("Lohri", "Lohri is a popular winter festival celebrated mainly in Punjab, Haryana, Himachal Pradesh and Jammu on the evening of 13 January, a day before Makar Sankranti. It marks the end of the coldest part of winter and the ripening of the rabi crops, especially sugarcane and wheat. In the evening, families and neighbours gather around a large bonfire in an open space. They offer sesame seeds, jaggery, popcorn, peanuts and rewri to the fire and pray for a good harvest and prosperity. People sing folk songs, and the beat of the dhol sets everyone dancing bhangra and gidda around the fire. Children go from house to house singing traditional songs, including the famous tale of Dulla Bhatti, a local hero who is said to have helped poor girls get married, and they receive sweets and money in return. Lohri is especially important for families celebrating the first year of a newborn child or a newly married couple. The festival spreads warmth, joy and togetherness in the cold winter."),
("Emperor Ashoka", "Ashoka was one of the greatest rulers in the history of India. He belonged to the Maurya dynasty and was the grandson of Chandragupta Maurya. He became emperor around 268 BCE and ruled over a vast empire that covered most of the Indian subcontinent. About eight years after his coronation, Ashoka fought a terrible war to conquer Kalinga, in present day Odisha. The war caused enormous loss of life and suffering, and the sight of this destruction filled him with deep regret. After the Kalinga war, he gave up the policy of conquest by war and adopted the path of dhamma, which stressed non violence, tolerance, kindness to all living beings and respect for elders. He spread these ideas through edicts carved on rocks and pillars across his empire. He also built hospitals for people and animals, planted trees along roads and sent missionaries to other lands to spread Buddhism. The Lion Capital of his pillar at Sarnath is now the national emblem of India, and the Ashoka Chakra appears on the national flag."),
]
